ML_PAGE("l5-notes", "<header class=\"page-header theme-l5\"><span class=\"eyebrow\">LECTURE 5 · COMPLETE REFERENCE</span><h2>Full Intuitive Explanation</h2><p>The complete Lecture 5 narrative, preserved in full. Use the guided lessons for a first pass; open this when a concept still isn't clicking.</p></header><div class=\"action-row\"><button class=\"primary-btn bg-l5\" data-action=\"print\">Print this page</button><button class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l5-subjective\">Go to subjective prep</button><button class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l5-prep\">Go to MCQ prep</button></div><details class=\"topic-index toc-panel theme-l5\" id=\"l5n-toc\"><summary><strong>Reading order</strong><span class=\"toc-count\">36 sections</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><input type=\"search\" class=\"toc-search\" placeholder=\"Filter sections...\" aria-label=\"Filter sections\"><ul class=\"toc-list\"><li data-full=\"Lecture 5 - Feature Scaling and Selection\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5n-s0\" title=\"Lecture 5 - Feature Scaling and Selection\">Chapter overview</button></li><li class=\"toc-group\">Part A - Feature Scaling</li><li data-full=\"1. Why do we even need feature scaling?\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5n-s2\" title=\"1. Why do we even need feature scaling?\"><span class=\"toc-no\">1</span>Distance dominance</button></li><li data-full=\"2. Which algorithms actually need scaling?\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5n-s3\" title=\"2. Which algorithms actually need scaling?\"><span class=\"toc-no\">2</span>Which models need scaling</button></li><li data-full=\"3. The three scaling techniques in this chapter\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5n-s4\" title=\"3. The three scaling techniques in this chapter\"><span class=\"toc-no\">3</span>The three scalers</button></li><li data-full=\"4. StandardScaler - how unusual is a value relative to the mean?\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5n-s5\" title=\"4. StandardScaler - how unusual is a value relative to the mean?\"><span class=\"toc-no\">4</span>StandardScaler (z-score)</button></li><li data-full=\"5. MinMaxScaler - position a value within a range\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5n-s6\" title=\"5. MinMaxScaler - position a value within a range\"><span class=\"toc-no\">5</span>MinMaxScaler</button></li><li data-full=\"6. Why Min-Max scaling struggles with outliers\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5n-s7\" title=\"6. Why Min-Max scaling struggles with outliers\"><span class=\"toc-no\">6</span>Min-max and outliers</button></li><li data-full=\"7. RobustScaler - use statistics that resist outliers\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5n-s8\" title=\"7. RobustScaler - use statistics that resist outliers\"><span class=\"toc-no\">7</span>RobustScaler</button></li><li data-full=\"8. Comparing the three scalers\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5n-s9\" title=\"8. Comparing the three scalers\"><span class=\"toc-no\">8</span>Comparing the scalers</button></li><li data-full=\"Part B - Feature Selection\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5n-s10\" title=\"Part B - Feature Selection\">Feature selection: why</button></li><li data-full=\"9. Do we actually need every feature?\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5n-s11\" title=\"9. Do we actually need every feature?\"><span class=\"toc-no\">9</span>Irrelevant and redundant features</button></li><li data-full=\"10. Feature engineering vs selection vs dimensionality reduction\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5n-s12\" title=\"10. Feature engineering vs selection vs dimensionality reduction\"><span class=\"toc-no\">10</span>Selection vs engineering vs PCA</button></li><li data-full=\"11. The curse of dimensionality\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5n-s13\" title=\"11. The curse of dimensionality\"><span class=\"toc-no\">11</span>Curse of dimensionality</button></li><li data-full=\"12. Three approaches to feature selection\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5n-s14\" title=\"12. Three approaches to feature selection\"><span class=\"toc-no\">12</span>Filter, wrapper, embedded</button></li><li class=\"toc-group\">Part C - Filter Methods</li><li data-full=\"13. Filter method 1: threshold-based selection\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5n-s16\" title=\"13. Filter method 1: threshold-based selection\"><span class=\"toc-no\">13</span>Thresholds (min_df, max_df)</button></li><li data-full=\"14. Filter method 2: variance threshold\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5n-s17\" title=\"14. Filter method 2: variance threshold\"><span class=\"toc-no\">14</span>VarianceThreshold</button></li><li data-full=\"15. Filter method 3: statistical feature selection\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5n-s18\" title=\"15. Filter method 3: statistical feature selection\"><span class=\"toc-no\">15</span>Chi-square, ANOVA, MI</button></li><li data-full=\"16. The major weakness of univariate statistical selection\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5n-s19\" title=\"16. The major weakness of univariate statistical selection\"><span class=\"toc-no\">16</span>Interactions get missed</button></li><li data-full=\"17. The Wisconsin Breast Cancer example\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5n-s20\" title=\"17. The Wisconsin Breast Cancer example\"><span class=\"toc-no\">17</span>Wisconsin 30 vs 15 features</button></li><li class=\"toc-group\">Part D - Wrapper Methods</li><li data-full=\"18. Why do we need wrapper methods?\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5n-s22\" title=\"18. Why do we need wrapper methods?\"><span class=\"toc-no\">18</span>Why wrapper methods</button></li><li data-full=\"19. Recursive Feature Elimination (RFE)\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5n-s23\" title=\"19. Recursive Feature Elimination (RFE)\"><span class=\"toc-no\">19</span>RFE step by step</button></li><li data-full=\"20. Why do Chi-Square and RFE select different features?\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5n-s24\" title=\"20. Why do Chi-Square and RFE select different features?\"><span class=\"toc-no\">20</span>Why chi-square and RFE disagree</button></li><li class=\"toc-group\">Part E - Embedded Methods</li><li data-full=\"21. What makes embedded selection different?\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5n-s26\" title=\"21. What makes embedded selection different?\"><span class=\"toc-no\">21</span>Embedded selection</button></li><li data-full=\"22. Random Forest feature importance\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5n-s27\" title=\"22. Random Forest feature importance\"><span class=\"toc-no\">22</span>Forest importance ≠ causality</button></li><li class=\"toc-group\">Part F - Comparing Feature Selection Methods</li><li data-full=\"23. Filter vs Wrapper vs Embedded\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5n-s29\" title=\"23. Filter vs Wrapper vs Embedded\"><span class=\"toc-no\">23</span>Comparing the three families</button></li><li class=\"toc-group\">Part G - Putting Scaling and Selection Together</li><li data-full=\"24. The single most important implementation rule: prevent leakage\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5n-s31\" title=\"24. The single most important implementation rule: prevent leakage\"><span class=\"toc-no\">24</span>Fit on training data only</button></li><li data-full=\"25. One complete, correctly organized ML pipeline\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5n-s32\" title=\"25. One complete, correctly organized ML pipeline\"><span class=\"toc-no\">25</span>A leak-free Pipeline</button></li><li data-full=\"26. An important correction to the lecture&#x27;s example\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5n-s33\" title=\"26. An important correction to the lecture&#x27;s example\"><span class=\"toc-no\">26</span>StandardScaler + chi2 bug</button></li><li class=\"toc-group\">Part H - Applying the Chapter to Real Scenarios</li><li data-full=\"27. Scenario 1: House price prediction with KNN\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5n-s35\" title=\"27. Scenario 1: House price prediction with KNN\"><span class=\"toc-no\">27</span>Scenario: KNN house prices</button></li><li data-full=\"28. Scenario 2: Same data, but Random Forest\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5n-s36\" title=\"28. Scenario 2: Same data, but Random Forest\"><span class=\"toc-no\">28</span>Scenario: same data, forest</button></li><li data-full=\"29. Scenario 3: Extreme salary outliers\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5n-s37\" title=\"29. Scenario 3: Extreme salary outliers\"><span class=\"toc-no\">29</span>Scenario: salary outliers</button></li><li data-full=\"30. Scenario 4: Thousands of text features\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5n-s38\" title=\"30. Scenario 4: Thousands of text features\"><span class=\"toc-no\">30</span>Scenario: 10,000 text features</button></li><li data-full=\"31. Scenario 5: A feature has zero variance\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5n-s39\" title=\"31. Scenario 5: A feature has zero variance\"><span class=\"toc-no\">31</span>Scenario: zero variance</button></li><li class=\"toc-group\">Part I - Preparing Your Understanding for the Exam</li><li data-full=\"32. High-value distinctions\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5n-s41\" title=\"32. High-value distinctions\"><span class=\"toc-no\">32</span>Key distinctions</button></li><li data-full=\"33. Check whether you really understand\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5n-s42\" title=\"33. Check whether you really understand\"><span class=\"toc-no\">33</span>Self-check questions</button></li><li data-full=\"34. The entire chapter in one mental model\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5n-s43\" title=\"34. The entire chapter in one mental model\"><span class=\"toc-no\">34</span>One mental model</button></li></ul></details><details class=\"full-notes theme-l5\" open><summary><div><strong>Lecture 5 - complete intuitive explanation</strong><small>Every section of the notes, with code, maths and interactive visuals.</small></div><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"markdown-body\"><div class=\"notes-toolbar\"><button type=\"button\" data-sections=\"open\">Expand all</button><button type=\"button\" data-sections=\"close\">Collapse all</button><span>36 sections · click a heading to open it</span></div><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5n-s0\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Lecture 5 - Feature Scaling and Selection</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>TEB3123 Machine Learning · Uploaded slides: Chapter 4, 57 pages</p>\n<p>The previous lectures taught you how to take raw data and turn it into useful features.</p>\n<p>Now imagine you've done everything correctly. You've cleaned your dataset, handled missing values, converted categories into numbers, and engineered additional features.</p>\n<p>But two problems remain:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Some features have much larger numerical values than others. Your algorithm might mistake numerical magnitude for importance.</li>\n<li>You might have too many features. Some are useful, some are irrelevant, and others repeat information already available.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>This chapter addresses these problems using two techniques:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Technique</th>\n<th>Question it answers</th>\n<th>What it does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Feature Scaling</strong></td>\n<td>How should numerical values be represented?</td>\n<td>Adjust numerical magnitude without removing features.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Feature Selection</strong></td>\n<td>Which features deserve to remain?</td>\n<td>Keep useful variables while removing unnecessary ones.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>This is exactly how the final slide distinguishes the two topics. Feature scaling deals with numerical range and magnitude, while feature selection deals with dimensionality, redundancy and retaining useful variables.</p>\n<p>We'll follow the slides, build the intuition behind each method, explain the mathematics, and apply everything to exam-style scenarios.</p></div></details><h2 class=\"notes-divider\" id=\"l5n-d1\">Part A - Feature Scaling</h2><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5n-s2\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">1. Why do we even need feature scaling?</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"l5_space3d\"><div class=\"vis-title\">Why scaling changes distances</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>3D · The same customers in raw units and in z-scores. Raw salary stretches one axis so far that age and experience vanish - and the nearest neighbour changes.</figcaption></figure></div><p>Imagine a bank wants to predict whether somebody is eligible for a loan.</p>\n<p>The dataset contains:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Feature</th>\n<th>Customer A</th>\n<th>Customer B</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Age</td>\n<td>25</td>\n<td>30</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Salary (RM)</td>\n<td>80,000</td>\n<td>85,000</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Experience (years)</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>5</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>To a human, these features mean different things.</p>\n<p>Age describes maturity. Salary describes financial capacity. Experience describes employment history.</p>\n<p>But a distance-based ML algorithm sees numbers.</p>\n<p>Let's use the Euclidean distance formula shown on page 13:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$d(A,B)=\\sqrt{\\sum_{j=1}^{n}(x_{Aj}-x_{Bj})^2}$$</div>\n<p>For the two customers:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$d=\\sqrt{(25-30)^2+(80000-85000)^2+(3-5)^2}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$d=\\sqrt{25+25,000,000+4}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{d\\approx5000}$$</div>\n<p>Now look at the contribution of each feature.</p>\n<p><strong>Contribution to squared distance (before scaling)</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Feature</th>\n<th class=\"ta-r\">Contribution</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Salary</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">25,000,000</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Age</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">25</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Experience</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">4</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The salary contribution is one million times the age contribution in this example, purely because of numerical scale.</p>\n<p>Does that mean salary is a million times more important than age?</p>\n<p>Absolutely not.</p>\n<p>The algorithm has confused numerical units with actual predictive relevance.</p>\n<p>This is what the lecture calls distance dominance.</p>\n<h4>What scaling fixes</h4>\n<p>Feature scaling transforms the numbers so that different variables operate on more comparable numerical scales.</p>\n<p>For example, instead of:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Age       = 25\nSalary    = 80000\nExperience= 3\n</code></pre>\n<p>we might have:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Age       = 0.25\nSalary    = 0.60\nExperience= 0.30\n</code></pre>\n<p>These are illustrative scaled values, not values calculated from a particular training dataset.</p>\n<p>Now salary no longer dominates the distance simply because it is measured in ringgit.</p>\n<p>Important: Scaling doesn't force all features to be equally informative. It removes an arbitrary advantage arising from their units.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5n-s3\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">2. Which algorithms actually need scaling?</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>One of the most useful slides is page 14, which distinguishes algorithms that are sensitive to scale from those that generally aren't.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Scale-sensitive</th>\n<th>Why</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>KNN</td>\n<td>Uses distances between observations</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>K-Means</td>\n<td>Groups observations based on distance</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>SVM</td>\n<td>Sensitive to feature magnitude, especially in distance-based kernels</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Linear/Logistic Regression</td>\n<td>Scaling often improves optimization and regularization behavior</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Neural Networks</td>\n<td>Can improve gradient-based training</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>PCA</td>\n<td>Large-variance features can dominate the extracted components</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>In contrast:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Generally less sensitive</th>\n<th>Why</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Decision Tree</td>\n<td>Uses feature thresholds</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Random Forest</td>\n<td>Combines decision trees</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>XGBoost / LightGBM (tree-based)</td>\n<td>Generally split by feature ordering and thresholds</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Think about the mechanism.</p>\n<p>KNN asks: How far apart are these observations?</p>\n<p>Decision Tree asks: Is income greater than this threshold?</p>\n<p>If you multiply every income by 1,000, KNN's distance calculations can change dramatically.</p>\n<p>But a decision tree can usually adjust its income split threshold accordingly without fundamentally changing the partition.</p>\n<h4>Likely subjective question</h4>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Explain why feature scaling is important for KNN but generally unnecessary for Random Forest.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Model answer:</strong></p>\n<p>KNN is a distance-based algorithm that measures similarity between observations using numerical distances. Features with large numerical ranges can therefore dominate the distance calculation even when they are not more important to the prediction task. Feature scaling reduces this problem by placing the variables on comparable scales. In contrast, Random Forest uses decision-tree splits based on thresholds within individual features. These splits are generally unaffected by consistent changes in numerical scale, making scaling much less necessary.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5n-s4\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">3. The three scaling techniques in this chapter</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The chapter focuses on:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>StandardScaler (Z-Score)</strong> - Uses mean and standard deviation.</li>\n<li><strong>MinMaxScaler</strong> - Uses minimum and maximum.</li>\n<li><strong>RobustScaler</strong> - Uses median and interquartile range.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>They all change numerical scales, but they use different reference points. That difference becomes especially important when your data contains outliers.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5n-s5\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">4. StandardScaler - how unusual is a value relative to the mean?</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>StandardScaler uses:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{z=\\frac{x-\\mu}{\\sigma}}$$</div>\n<p>where:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>$x$ = original value</li>\n<li>$\\mu$ = mean of the feature</li>\n<li>$\\sigma$ = standard deviation of the feature</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The transformed training feature has approximately:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$Mean=0,\\qquad StandardDeviation=1$$</div>\n<h4>Intuition</h4>\n<p>Imagine an exam where the class average is 50 marks and the standard deviation is 10.</p>\n<p>A student scores 60.</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$z=\\frac{60-50}{10}=1$$</div>\n<p>What does that mean?</p>\n<p>The student scored one standard deviation above the mean.</p>\n<p>A student who scores 30:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$z=\\frac{30-50}{10}=-2$$</div>\n<p>is two standard deviations below the mean.</p>\n<p>Notice that Z-scores can be negative.</p>\n<p>That's perfectly fine. Negative simply means below the mean.</p>\n<h4>What StandardScaler does well</h4>\n<p>It expresses different features in terms of their own mean and standard deviation.</p>\n<p>This is often useful for scale-sensitive algorithms such as SVM, regularized regression and PCA.</p>\n<h4>Its limitation</h4>\n<p>Mean and standard deviation can be distorted by extreme values.</p>\n<p>If one person earns RM10 million while everybody else earns RM50,000, the mean salary increases significantly.</p>\n<p>StandardScaler does not magically remove that influence.</p>\n<h4>Core code</h4>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">from sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler\n\nscaler = StandardScaler()\n\nscaler.fit(X_train)\n\nX_train_scaled = scaler.transform(X_train)\nX_test_scaled = scaler.transform(X_test)\n</code></pre>\n<p>Understand these three things:</p>\n<p>fit() learns the mean and standard deviation from the training data.</p>\n<p>transform() applies the learned Z-score formula.</p>\n<p>The test set uses the same mean and standard deviation, not newly calculated ones.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5n-s6\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">5. MinMaxScaler - position a value within a range</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Min-Max scaling is easier to visualize.</p>\n<p>The formula is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{x'=\\frac{x-x_{\\min}}{x_{\\max}-x_{\\min}}}$$</div>\n<p>The idea is:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Where does this number sit between the minimum and maximum?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Suppose:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$x_{\\min}=0,\\qquad x_{\\max}=100$$</div>\n<p>and:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$x=25$$</div>\n<p>Then:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$x'=\\frac{25-0}{100-0}=0.25$$</div>\n<p>Meaning: the number is 25% of the way through that numerical range.</p>\n<p>Notice:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$Minimum\\rightarrow0$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$Maximum\\rightarrow1$$</div>\n<p>and everything between them is proportionally positioned.</p>\n<h4>Why is this useful?</h4>\n<p>It is useful when a bounded numerical representation is desirable, such as normalizing certain numerical inputs or pixel intensities.</p>\n<p>But it has an important weakness.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5n-s7\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">6. Why Min-Max scaling struggles with outliers</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"l5_scalers\"><div class=\"vis-title\">Three scalers, one outlier</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Animated · The lecture&#x27;s video views under all three scalers. Make the viral video bigger and watch which scaler lets it squash the ordinary videos.</figcaption></figure></div><p>The lecture uses video view counts:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$[1,\\ 5,\\ 25,\\ 300,\\ 1295,\\ 19000]$$</div>\n<p>The maximum is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$19000$$</div>\n<p>The minimum is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$1$$</div>\n<p>Now consider the video with 1,295 views.</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$x'=\\frac{1295-1}{19000-1}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{x'\\approx0.0681}$$</div>\n<p>A video with 1,295 views is now only about 0.068 on the scaled range.</p>\n<p>Meanwhile, the 19,000-view video becomes 1.</p>\n<p><strong>Slide example: video views after Min-Max scaling</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Views</th>\n<th>Scaled value</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>0.0000</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>0.0002</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>25</td>\n<td>0.0013</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>300</td>\n<td>0.0157</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1,295</td>\n<td>0.0681</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>19,000</td>\n<td>1.0000</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The outlier uses almost the entire numerical range.</p>\n<p>All ordinary videos are squeezed toward zero.</p>\n<p>This is why the slides warns about MinMaxScaler's high sensitivity to outliers.</p>\n<p>Technical distinction: The [0,1] guarantee applies to values within the training range. A future observation outside that range can scale below 0 or above 1 unless clipping is explicitly enabled.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5n-s8\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">7. RobustScaler - use statistics that resist outliers</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Now suppose your dataset is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$[1,\\ 2,\\ 3,\\ 4,\\ 1000]$$</div>\n<p>The mean is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\frac{1+2+3+4+1000}{5}=202$$</div>\n<p>But look at the typical observations.</p>\n<p>They're:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$1,\\ 2,\\ 3,\\ 4$$</div>\n<p>A mean of 202 is a terrible description of a typical value here.</p>\n<p>The median, however, is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$3$$</div>\n<p>It remains stable despite the 1,000.</p>\n<p>RobustScaler uses:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{x'=\\frac{x-\\text{Median}(X)}{IQR(X)}}$$</div>\n<p>where:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$IQR=Q_3-Q_1$$</div>\n<p>and:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$Q_1=25th\\ percentile$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$Q_3=75th\\ percentile$$</div>\n<h4>Intuition</h4>\n<p>StandardScaler asks:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>How far are you from the mean, measured in standard deviations?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>RobustScaler asks:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>How far are you from the median, measured relative to the spread of the middle 50%?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Because the median and IQR focus on the central distribution, they are less affected by extreme observations.</p>\n<h4>Why this matters</h4>\n<p>For the video dataset, RobustScaler doesn't squeeze every normal view count nearly to zero to accommodate the 19,000-view video.</p>\n<p>The slides illustrates this comparison directly:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Raw views</th>\n<th>StandardScaler</th>\n<th>MinMaxScaler</th>\n<th>RobustScaler</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>-0.49</td>\n<td>0.0000</td>\n<td>-0.16</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>300</td>\n<td>-0.44</td>\n<td>0.0157</td>\n<td>0.14</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1,295</td>\n<td>-0.30</td>\n<td>0.0681</td>\n<td>1.11</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>19,000</td>\n<td>+2.23</td>\n<td>1.0000</td>\n<td>18.43</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Values rounded as presented on slide 27.</p>\n<p>The key lesson is that RobustScaler provides a more readable spread for the ordinary observations.</p>\n<p>Very important: RobustScaler does not delete, truncate or fix outliers. The 19,000-view observation remains an extreme outlier, which is why its robust-scaled value is still very large.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5n-s9\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">8. Comparing the three scalers</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>StandardScaler</th>\n<th>MinMaxScaler</th>\n<th>RobustScaler</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Reference</td>\n<td>Mean</td>\n<td>Minimum</td>\n<td>Median</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Scale factor</td>\n<td>Standard deviation</td>\n<td>Maximum - minimum</td>\n<td>IQR</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Typical output</td>\n<td>Mean 0, SD 1</td>\n<td>0 to 1 (training range)</td>\n<td>Median 0, IQR 1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Outlier sensitivity</td>\n<td>Moderate/high</td>\n<td>Very high</td>\n<td>Lower</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Typical use</td>\n<td>General scale-sensitive algorithms</td>\n<td>Bounded-range representation</td>\n<td>Heavily skewed/outlier-prone features</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h4>How I would choose</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Reasonably distributed numerical features, no major outlier problem</strong> - Start by considering StandardScaler.</li>\n<li><strong>Need features in a specified bounded range</strong> - Consider MinMaxScaler, provided extreme values are understood.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong outliers distort mean/min/max</strong> - Consider RobustScaler.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This reflects the decision guide on page 28. These are starting rules, not guarantees that one method will produce the highest model accuracy.</p>\n<h4>Likely subjective question</h4>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Compare StandardScaler, MinMaxScaler and RobustScaler, including their treatment of outliers.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Model answer:</strong></p>\n<p>StandardScaler transforms each feature by subtracting its mean and dividing by its standard deviation, producing values centered around zero with unit standard deviation. MinMaxScaler transforms values based on their minimum and maximum, typically mapping the training range to zero and one. RobustScaler instead uses the median and interquartile range, making its scaling parameters less sensitive to extreme observations. Therefore, StandardScaler is a common general-purpose choice for scale-sensitive models, MinMaxScaler is useful when a bounded range is required, and RobustScaler is preferable to consider when the feature distribution contains substantial outliers. However, robust scaling does not remove outliers, and the final choice should be evaluated against model performance.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5n-s10\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part B - Feature Selection</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>We have dealt with numerical magnitude. Now the chapter moves to a different problem.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5n-s11\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">9. Do we actually need every feature?</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Imagine building a model to predict whether a student will pass an exam.</p>\n<p>You collect:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Attendance\nPrevious GPA\nStudy hours\nAssignment scores\nStudent ID\nFavourite colour\nShoe size\nPrevious GPA converted to percentage\n</code></pre>\n<p>Eight features.</p>\n<p>Are all eight useful?</p>\n<p>Probably not.</p>\n<p>The student ID may just be an identifier. Favourite colour and shoe size are unlikely to have meaningful predictive value.</p>\n<p>Also notice:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Previous GPA\nPrevious GPA converted to percentage\n</code></pre>\n<p>Both largely represent the same information.</p>\n<p>This introduces two important problems:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Irrelevance: a feature contributes little useful information.</li>\n<li>Redundancy: multiple features provide overlapping information.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Feature selection tries to solve both.</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\nOriginal\\ Features\n\\rightarrow\nSelect\\ Useful\\ Subset\n\\rightarrow\nModel\n}$$</div>\n<p>The lecture defines feature selection as identifying informative variables while removing noise and redundancy.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5n-s12\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">10. Feature engineering vs selection vs dimensionality reduction</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The slides makes a distinction worth remembering.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Technique</th>\n<th>What happens?</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Feature Engineering</td>\n<td>Creates useful features</td>\n<td>Income and Debt → Debt-to-Income</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Feature Selection</td>\n<td>Keeps some original columns</td>\n<td>Keep Age and Income, remove Shoe Size</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Dimensionality Reduction / Extraction</td>\n<td>Constructs new representation axes</td>\n<td>PCA transforms several variables into principal components</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Think of a student dataset.</p>\n<p>Feature engineering:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$StudyHoursPerWeek=\\frac{TotalStudyHours}{Weeks}$$</div>\n<p>Feature selection:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Keep Attendance, GPA, StudyHours\nRemove StudentID, ShoeSize\n</code></pre>\n<p>PCA-style dimensionality reduction:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">GPA + Assignment + Test scores\n            ↓\n     Principal Component 1\n</code></pre>\n<p>Selection keeps original variables. PCA-style extraction creates new ones.</p>\n<p>Why does this matter?</p>\n<p>Because retained original variables are easier to explain.</p>\n<p>A supervisor can understand:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Attendance was important in predicting student performance.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>A synthetic principal component may be much harder to explain in everyday terms.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5n-s13\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">11. The curse of dimensionality</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"l5_curse3d\"><div class=\"vis-title\">The curse of dimensionality</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>3D · How big must a neighbourhood be to hold 10% of the data? Add features and watch the box swallow the whole cube.</figcaption></figure></div><p>This sounds complicated but the underlying idea is straightforward.</p>\n<p>Imagine you have observations described using just two features:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$X_1=Age,\\qquad X_2=Income$$</div>\n<p>You can plot them in a two-dimensional space.</p>\n<p>Now imagine adding:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Location\nEducation\nEmployment\nTransaction history\nProduct preferences\nWebsite behaviour\n...\n</code></pre>\n<p>Suddenly you have hundreds or thousands of dimensions.</p>\n<p>The space of possible combinations grows enormously.</p>\n<p>The lecture calls this the curse of dimensionality.</p>\n<h4>Why can too many dimensions be problematic?</h4>\n<p>Suppose each feature has 10 possible values.</p>\n<p>With one feature:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$10$$</div>\n<p>possible combinations.</p>\n<p>With two:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$10^2=100$$</div>\n<p>With ten:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$10^{10}$$</div>\n<p>possible combinations.</p>\n<p>The data becomes increasingly sparse relative to the space.</p>\n<p>Your model may struggle to find enough comparable examples, particularly for distance-based algorithms.</p>\n<p>Too many features can also increase computation, encourage overfitting, and reduce interpretability.</p>\n<p>However, more features are not inherently bad. If additional variables contain genuinely useful information, they may improve the model. The problem is adding dimensions that create cost, noise or redundancy without sufficient predictive benefit.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5n-s14\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">12. Three approaches to feature selection</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"l5_families\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>Filter methods score features once; wrappers refit a model in a loop; embedded methods get importance while training.</figcaption></figure></div><p>The lecture divides feature selection into three families:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Filter Methods</strong> - Select features using statistical measures or thresholds, without repeatedly training the final predictive model.</li>\n<li><strong>Wrapper Methods</strong> - Repeatedly train a model with different feature subsets and use the model to guide selection.</li>\n<li><strong>Embedded Methods</strong> - Use feature importance or selection behavior that arises during the model's own fitting process.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>This is the chapter's central classification for feature selection.</p>\n<p>Here's an intuitive analogy.</p>\n<p>Imagine hiring employees.</p>\n<p>A filter method screens applications using qualifications and preset criteria.</p>\n<p>A wrapper method repeatedly tests different teams of applicants and evaluates which team performs best.</p>\n<p>An embedded method observes a model's assessment of contributions during its normal training process.</p>\n<p>Same general objective: identify useful candidates.</p>\n<p>Different approaches and computational costs.</p></div></details><h2 class=\"notes-divider\" id=\"l5n-d15\">Part C - Filter Methods</h2><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5n-s16\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">13. Filter method 1: threshold-based selection</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The simplest method is to define a cutoff.</p>\n<p>Imagine we are building a text classifier.</p>\n<p>The corpus contains 10,000 documents.</p>\n<p>Some words appear in only one document.</p>\n<p>Others appear in almost every document.</p>\n<p>Think:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">&quot;the&quot; → appears nearly everywhere\n&quot;an_obscure_typo&quot; → appears only once\n</code></pre>\n<p>Both might be poor general-purpose features.</p>\n<p>So we set thresholds.</p>\n<p>The lecture's example uses:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">from sklearn.feature_extraction.text import CountVectorizer\n\nvectorizer = CountVectorizer(\n    min_df=0.10,\n    max_df=0.85,\n    max_features=2000\n)\n</code></pre>\n<p>Interpret:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>min_df=0.10: reject terms appearing in fewer than 10% of documents.</li>\n<li>max_df=0.85: reject terms appearing in more than 85% of documents.</li>\n<li>max_features=2000: limit the vocabulary to at most 2,000 selected terms.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The slides give this example on pages 35-36.</p>\n<h4>Why is it useful?</h4>\n<p>Because the representation becomes smaller.</p>\n<p>You remove extremely rare and excessively common features according to your chosen rules.</p>\n<p>But think critically: a rare term isn't automatically useless.</p>\n<p>For fraud detection, a rare phrase could be extremely informative.</p>\n<p>So thresholds should reflect the task, not arbitrary assumptions.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5n-s17\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">14. Filter method 2: variance threshold</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"l5_variance\"><div class=\"vis-title\">VarianceThreshold and its blind spot</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · Slide the variance threshold over the lecture&#x27;s genetic markers - then reveal which feature actually predicts fraud.</figcaption></figure></div><p>This is probably the most intuitive feature-selection method in the chapter.</p>\n<p>Imagine a column:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Has_Nationality = Malaysian\n\nRow 1 → 1\nRow 2 → 1\nRow 3 → 1\nRow 4 → 1\nRow 5 → 1\n</code></pre>\n<p>Every observation has the same value.</p>\n<p>Can that feature distinguish one observation from another within this dataset?</p>\n<p>No.</p>\n<p>Its variance is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$Variance=0$$</div>\n<p>A constant feature cannot explain variation between observations.</p>\n<p>Therefore, it can usually be removed.</p>\n<h4>The chapter's genetic marker example</h4>\n<p>The lecture uses six binary genetic markers:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Gen 1\nGen 2\nGen 3\nGen 4\nGen 5\nGen 6\n</code></pre>\n<p>and applies:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$VarianceThreshold=0.15$$</div>\n<p>Only:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{Gen\\ 1,\\ Gen\\ 3,\\ Gen\\ 5}$$</div>\n<p>are retained because their variances exceed the threshold.</p>\n<p>See the code and selected-feature table on pages 37-38.</p>\n<h4>Core code</h4>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">from sklearn.feature_selection import VarianceThreshold\n\nselector = VarianceThreshold(threshold=0.15)\n\nX_train_selected = selector.fit_transform(X_train)\nX_test_selected = selector.transform(X_test)\n</code></pre>\n<p>This is important:</p>\n<p>VarianceThreshold is unsupervised feature selection.</p>\n<p>It doesn't need the target labels $y$.</p>\n<p>It only examines variation in $X$.</p>\n<h4>Its limitation</h4>\n<p>A feature with low variance can still be very useful.</p>\n<p>Suppose:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">99% of transactions → no unusual flag\n 1% of transactions → unusual flag\n</code></pre>\n<p>That flag has low variance, but it could be important for detecting rare fraud.</p>\n<p>VarianceThreshold may discard it without ever checking whether it predicts fraud.</p>\n<p>Therefore:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Variance-based selection is computationally simple, but it does not directly measure predictive relevance.</p>\n</blockquote></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5n-s18\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">15. Filter method 3: statistical feature selection</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Now we're doing something more intelligent than simply checking whether a column varies.</p>\n<p>We ask:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Is this feature statistically related to the target we're trying to predict?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The lecture introduces three approaches:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Method</th>\n<th>Main intuition</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Chi-Square ($\\chi^2$)</td>\n<td>Measure dependence between non-negative features and categorical target</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ANOVA F-Test</td>\n<td>Assess differences in a continuous feature across target classes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Mutual Information</td>\n<td>Measure statistical dependency, including nonlinear relationships</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>These are the three scoring approaches displayed on page 40.</p>\n<h4>Chi-Square</h4>\n<p>Suppose:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Feature: Owns_House\nTarget: Loan_Default\n</code></pre>\n<p>You want to know whether home ownership and loan default are statistically associated.</p>\n<p>Chi-Square can help test for dependence.</p>\n<p>The key idea:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$Feature \\leftrightarrow Target$$</div>\n<p>Higher relevant scores suggest stronger evidence of association under that scoring method.</p>\n<h4>ANOVA</h4>\n<p>Suppose:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Feature: Income\nTarget: Default / No Default\n</code></pre>\n<p>We want to know whether income levels differ meaningfully between the two classes.</p>\n<p>ANOVA compares variation between groups relative to variation within groups.</p>\n<h4>Mutual Information</h4>\n<p>Mutual information asks:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>How much information about the target does knowing this feature provide?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>This can capture dependencies that are not simply linear.</p>\n<p>For subjective questions, the important point is that these methods evaluate features individually against the target.</p>\n<p>That gives them an advantage over simple variance thresholding: they directly consider the prediction objective.</p>\n<p>But it creates another weakness.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5n-s19\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">16. The major weakness of univariate statistical selection</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Imagine two features:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$X_1,\\ X_2$$</div>\n<p>Individually, neither strongly predicts the outcome.</p>\n<p>But together:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$X_1\\times X_2$$</div>\n<p>is very informative.</p>\n<p>A univariate method that evaluates each original feature in isolation might discard both.</p>\n<p>This is the essential limitation of many filter methods.</p>\n<p>They can miss feature interactions.</p>\n<p>That is one reason the lecture introduces wrapper methods later.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5n-s20\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">17. The Wisconsin Breast Cancer example</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"l5_filter\"><div class=\"vis-title\">SelectKBest with chi-square</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · Real chi-square scores for the 30 Wisconsin features. Choose k and see what SelectKBest keeps and how accuracy responds.</figcaption></figure></div><p>This is one of the most exam-relevant examples because the lecture gives actual model performance.</p>\n<p>The dataset contains:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th class=\"ta-r\">Samples</th>\n<th class=\"ta-r\">Numeric features</th>\n<th class=\"ta-r\">Classes</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">569</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">30</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">2</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The features describe characteristics of cell nuclei, such as radius, texture, perimeter and area.</p>\n<p>The lecture applies Chi-Square-based SelectKBest to retain 15 of the original 30 features.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">from sklearn.feature_selection import SelectKBest, chi2\n\nselector = SelectKBest(score_func=chi2, k=15)\nX_selected = selector.fit_transform(X, y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>The syntax expresses a simple idea:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Evaluate all 30 features against the target and retain the 15 highest-ranked features.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The slides report the following model accuracies:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Features used</th>\n<th class=\"ta-r\">Reported accuracy</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>All 30 features</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">95.09%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Selected 15 features</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">95.26%</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Source: lecture pages 42-45.</p>\n<p>What should you conclude?</p>\n<p>The slides show that reducing the feature count by 50% produced a small reported improvement in accuracy:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$95.26-95.09=\\boxed{0.17\\ percentage\\ points}$$</div>\n<p>That's a useful demonstration that more features do not always mean better model performance.</p>\n<p>But 0.17 percentage points is a small difference. By itself, it doesn't establish that the reduced model is reliably superior under repeated evaluation or on a completely unseen dataset.</p>\n<p>There is also an evaluation detail in the slide's code: selecting features before performing cross-validation can leak selection information between folds. The rigorous approach is to perform feature selection separately within each training fold.</p>\n<p>So the lecture's result is useful as an illustration, not conclusive proof that dropping half the features improves generalization.</p></div></details><h2 class=\"notes-divider\" id=\"l5n-d21\">Part D - Wrapper Methods</h2><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5n-s22\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">18. Why do we need wrapper methods?</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Recall the weakness of filter methods.</p>\n<p>Many filters evaluate features one at a time.</p>\n<p>They might say:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Feature A → score 0.8\nFeature B → score 0.6\nFeature C → score 0.4\n</code></pre>\n<p>Then retain A and B.</p>\n<p>But what if A and C work particularly well together?</p>\n<p>A wrapper method uses a predictive model to evaluate feature subsets in a model-dependent way.</p>\n<p>This can capture some interaction and redundancy effects that simple univariate ranking misses.</p>\n<p>The trade-off is computation.</p>\n<p>You must fit models repeatedly.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5n-s23\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">19. Recursive Feature Elimination (RFE)</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"l5_rfe\"><div class=\"vis-title\">Recursive feature elimination</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Animated · Recursive feature elimination on the Wisconsin data, one refit and one dropped feature at a time, then compared with chi-square.</figcaption></figure></div><p>The lecture introduces RFE as its principal wrapper method.</p>\n<p>The procedure is:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Fit</strong> - Train an estimator using the currently retained features.</li>\n<li><strong>Rank</strong> - Evaluate feature importance using the estimator.</li>\n<li><strong>Eliminate</strong> - Remove the lowest-ranked feature or features.</li>\n<li><strong>Repeat</strong> - Continue until the specified number of features remains.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>For example:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$30\\rightarrow29\\rightarrow28\\rightarrow\\cdots\\rightarrow15$$</div>\n<p>That is the workflow presented on page 48.</p>\n<h4>Core code</h4>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">from sklearn.feature_selection import RFE\nfrom sklearn.linear_model import LogisticRegression\n\nmodel = LogisticRegression(max_iter=1000)\n\nselector = RFE(\n    estimator=model,\n    n_features_to_select=15,\n    step=1\n)\n\nselector.fit(X_train, y_train)\n</code></pre>\n<p>The key things to recognize:</p>\n<p>estimator=model means we use that model to evaluate feature importance.</p>\n<p>n_features_to_select=15 specifies the final subset size.</p>\n<p>step=1 removes one feature at each iteration.</p>\n<h4>Why is RFE slower?</h4>\n<p>Because it repeatedly fits the model.</p>\n<p>If you have 10,000 text features and remove them one at a time, that can become computationally expensive.</p>\n<p>The lecture specifically warns against using RFE as the first technique on very high-dimensional text data.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5n-s24\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">20. Why do Chi-Square and RFE select different features?</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Here's an interesting result from the lecture.</p>\n<p>Both methods select 15 features from the same breast-cancer dataset.</p>\n<p>However, only 8 of those 15 selected features overlap.</p>\n<p>Why?</p>\n<p>Because they use different selection logic.</p>\n<h4>Chi-Square</h4>\n<p>Primarily evaluates:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$Feature_i\\leftrightarrow Target$$</div>\n<p>individually.</p>\n<h4>RFE</h4>\n<p>Repeatedly fits a model and evaluates feature contributions in the context of the current feature subset.</p>\n<p>A feature may have an impressive individual statistical association but contribute relatively little additional predictive information after another correlated feature is already included.</p>\n<p>Conversely, a feature may become useful in combination with other features.</p>\n<p>Therefore:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Different feature-selection methods can reasonably return different subsets because they define usefulness differently.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>This is a strong subjective-exam point.</p>\n<p>However: RFE only captures relationships that its underlying estimator can represent. Using Logistic Regression does not automatically give RFE the ability to discover arbitrary nonlinear interactions.</p></div></details><h2 class=\"notes-divider\" id=\"l5n-d25\">Part E - Embedded Methods</h2><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5n-s26\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">21. What makes embedded selection different?</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Remember:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Filter: evaluate statistical criteria without training the final model repeatedly.</li>\n<li>Wrapper: repeatedly fit models to evaluate feature subsets.</li>\n<li>Embedded: let the model's own training process produce information about useful features.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The lecture uses Random Forest as its main example.</p>\n<p>A Random Forest consists of multiple decision trees.</p>\n<p>During training, the trees use features to split observations into groups.</p>\n<p>Some features repeatedly produce more useful splits than others.</p>\n<p>The model can then estimate feature importance.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5n-s27\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">22. Random Forest feature importance</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"l5_importance\"><div class=\"vis-title\">Embedded selection: forest importance</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · Random-forest impurity importance on the Wisconsin data, by feature and by feature family.</figcaption></figure></div><p>The lecture demonstrates:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">from sklearn.ensemble import RandomForestClassifier\n\nmodel = RandomForestClassifier(random_state=42)\nmodel.fit(X_train, y_train)\n\nimportance = model.feature_importances_\n</code></pre>\n<p>Now importance contains one importance score for each input feature.</p>\n<p>For instance, the slide on the slides shows a feature-importance chart where characteristics such as Worst Area, Worst Radius and Worst Concavity rank among the most important.</p>\n<p>The particular importance scores arise from the model's tree-based impurity reductions.</p>\n<h4>What does a high score mean?</h4>\n<p>Broadly:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>The feature made a substantial contribution to impurity reduction across the fitted trees.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>A high score suggests that the fitted model relies strongly on that feature when making splits.</p>\n<p>But it does not necessarily mean:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>This feature causes the outcome.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>That's a very important distinction.</p>\n<p>For example, ice-cream sales may predict hot-weather-related events, but that doesn't prove ice cream caused the events.</p>\n<p>Predictive importance is not causality.</p>\n<p>The lecture explicitly warns against making that interpretation.</p></div></details><h2 class=\"notes-divider\" id=\"l5n-d28\">Part F - Comparing Feature Selection Methods</h2><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5n-s29\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">23. Filter vs Wrapper vs Embedded</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>This table is worth learning for both MCQ and subjective exams.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Criterion</th>\n<th>Filter</th>\n<th>Wrapper</th>\n<th>Embedded</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Uses predictive model?</td>\n<td>Usually no</td>\n<td>Yes, repeatedly</td>\n<td>Yes, during fitting</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Typical speed</td>\n<td>Fast</td>\n<td>Slow</td>\n<td>Moderate</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Uses target labels?</td>\n<td>Sometimes</td>\n<td>Usually</td>\n<td>Usually</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Feature interactions</td>\n<td>Often missed in simple univariate filters</td>\n<td>Can capture model-dependent effects</td>\n<td>Can capture model-dependent effects</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Example</td>\n<td>VarianceThreshold, Chi-Square</td>\n<td>RFE</td>\n<td>Random Forest importance, Lasso</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Strength</td>\n<td>Efficient, straightforward</td>\n<td>Tailored to model</td>\n<td>Integrates assessment with training</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Limitation</td>\n<td>May overlook joint effects</td>\n<td>Computational expense</td>\n<td>Model-dependent importance</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h4>Which should you choose?</h4>\n<p>Imagine you have:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$10,000\\ text\\ features$$</div>\n<p>Would you immediately use RFE?</p>\n<p>Probably not.</p>\n<p>The lecture recommends using faster filtering techniques first.</p>\n<p>A sensible workflow is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$10,000\\ features$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\downarrow$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$Filter\\ Methods$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\downarrow$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$Smaller\\ Candidate\\ Set$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\downarrow$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$Optional\\ Wrapper/Embedded\\ Selection$$</div>\n<p>This reduces computation.</p>\n<p>But don't assume that every additional feature-selection stage improves performance. The final subset still needs evaluation.</p></div></details><h2 class=\"notes-divider\" id=\"l5n-d30\">Part G - Putting Scaling and Selection Together</h2><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5n-s31\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">24. The single most important implementation rule: prevent leakage</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"l5_cv_pipeline\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>Scaling and selection learn from data, so they must be refitted on the training part of every fold.</figcaption></figure></div><p>This is a direct continuation of Lecture 2.</p>\n<p>The lecture's the slides states that scaling parameters and feature-selection rules must be learned from the training data only.</p>\n<p>Why?</p>\n<p>Because both preprocessing stages can learn information from data.</p>\n<p>Scaling learns statistics such as:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$Mean,\\ StandardDeviation,\\ Min,\\ Max,\\ Median,\\ IQR$$</div>\n<p>Selection may learn:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$Feature\\ variance,\\ ChiSquare\\ scores,\\ Model\\ importance$$</div>\n<p>If you fit these on the entire dataset before testing, test-set information has influenced your training process.</p>\n<p>That defeats the purpose of a genuinely unseen test set.</p>\n<h4>Wrong approach</h4>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">X_scaled = scaler.fit_transform(X)\nX_selected = selector.fit_transform(X_scaled, y)\n\n# Splitting only after transformations\n</code></pre>\n<p>Here the scaling and selection have already seen the full dataset.</p>\n<h4>Correct approach</h4>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\nSplit\\ First\n\\rightarrow\nFit\\ Scaling\\ on\\ Train\n\\rightarrow\nFit\\ Selection\\ on\\ Train\n\\rightarrow\nTrain\\ Model\n\\rightarrow\nEvaluate\\ on\\ Test\n}$$</div>\n<p>This anti-leakage rule appears explicitly in the lecture's pipeline section.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5n-s32\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">25. One complete, correctly organized ML pipeline</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Scikit-learn's Pipeline can apply the transformations and model as one ordered workflow.</p>\n<p>Here is the core implementation combining the chapter's techniques:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">from sklearn.pipeline import Pipeline\nfrom sklearn.preprocessing import MinMaxScaler\nfrom sklearn.feature_selection import SelectKBest, chi2\nfrom sklearn.linear_model import LogisticRegression\n\npipeline = Pipeline([\n    (&quot;scaler&quot;, MinMaxScaler()),\n    (&quot;selector&quot;, SelectKBest(score_func=chi2, k=15)),\n    (&quot;model&quot;, LogisticRegression(max_iter=1000))\n])\n\npipeline.fit(X_train, y_train)\n\naccuracy = pipeline.score(X_test, y_test)\nprint(accuracy)\n</code></pre>\n<p>Assume that X_train, X_test, y_train and y_test have already been created.</p>\n<p>Understand the flow:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Training Data\n     ↓\nMinMaxScaler\n     ↓\nSelectKBest (15 features)\n     ↓\nLogistic Regression\n     ↓\nTrained Pipeline\n     ↓\nUnseen Test Data\n     ↓\nAccuracy\n</code></pre>\n<p>The beauty of a pipeline is that preprocessing and modelling are managed together.</p>\n<p>When you call:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">pipeline.fit(X_train, y_train)\n</code></pre>\n<p>the scaler and selector learn from the training data.</p>\n<p>When you call:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">pipeline.score(X_test, y_test)\n</code></pre>\n<p>the test data passes through the already-fitted transformations.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5n-s33\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">26. An important correction to the lecture's example</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>On page 54, the slide combines:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">StandardScaler()\nSelectKBest(chi2)\nLogisticRegression()\n</code></pre>\n<p>There is a technical problem with this particular combination.</p>\n<p>Scikit-learn's chi2 selector requires non-negative feature values.</p>\n<p>StandardScaler produces negative values for observations below the mean.</p>\n<p>So that pipeline can fail when chi2 receives negative training features.</p>\n<p>That's why the working example above uses:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">MinMaxScaler()\n</code></pre>\n<p>before:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">SelectKBest(chi2)\n</code></pre>\n<p>Another approach would be to use a suitable scoring function such as f_classif instead of chi2 with standardized numerical data.</p>\n<p>This does not change the lecture's overall lesson about using pipelines. It corrects the specific choice of incompatible transformations.</p>\n<p>Also, when using cross-validation, the entire pipeline must be fitted inside each training fold, not once before cross-validation.</p></div></details><h2 class=\"notes-divider\" id=\"l5n-d34\">Part H - Applying the Chapter to Real Scenarios</h2><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5n-s35\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">27. Scenario 1: House price prediction with KNN</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>A housing dataset contains income (RM), number of bedrooms and building age. The model uses KNN to predict house prices. Explain whether scaling is necessary.</p>\n<p>Reason step by step.</p>\n<p>First, KNN uses distances.</p>\n<p>Second, the features have different numerical ranges:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Income       → tens of thousands of RM\nBedrooms     → typically single digits\nBuilding age → years\n</code></pre>\n<p>Without scaling, income could dominate the distance calculation.</p>\n<p>Therefore scaling is appropriate.</p>\n<h4>Strong subjective answer</h4>\n<p>KNN is a distance-based algorithm, so feature magnitude directly influences the similarity between observations. Income may have a much larger numerical range than bedrooms or building age, causing it to dominate Euclidean distance calculations. Feature scaling should therefore be considered to place the numerical variables on more comparable scales, ensuring that similarity is not determined simply by differences in measurement units.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5n-s36\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">28. Scenario 2: Same data, but Random Forest</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The same housing dataset is now modelled using Random Forest. Is scaling necessary?</p>\n<p>Usually not.</p>\n<p>Why?</p>\n<p>Random Forest uses decision-tree splits.</p>\n<p>A feature threshold such as:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$Income &gt; RM80,000$$</div>\n<p>can simply be adjusted to the corresponding transformed value if that variable is rescaled.</p>\n<p>The ranking and underlying splitting logic are generally preserved.</p>\n<p>Therefore, scaling is less important.</p>\n<p>But this does not mean all data preparation becomes unnecessary. Missing values, inappropriate encodings and poorly designed features can still cause problems.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5n-s37\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">29. Scenario 3: Extreme salary outliers</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>A bank has salary data ranging from RM2,000 to RM10 million. A few extremely wealthy customers distort the distribution. Which scaler would be suitable?</p>\n<p>The lecture's expected choice is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{RobustScaler}$$</div>\n<p>because:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$Median,\\ IQR$$</div>\n<p>are less sensitive to outliers than mean/standard deviation or minimum/maximum.</p>\n<p><strong>A strong answer:</strong></p>\n<p>RobustScaler is suitable to consider because the salary feature contains substantial outliers. Unlike StandardScaler, which uses the mean and standard deviation, or MinMaxScaler, which uses the minimum and maximum, RobustScaler centers observations using the median and scales them using the interquartile range. This limits the influence of extreme salaries on the scaling parameters and can preserve better numerical separation among the ordinary salary observations. However, the outliers remain present and should still be assessed for validity.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5n-s38\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">30. Scenario 4: Thousands of text features</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>A company has 10,000 text features and wants to reduce computational cost. Should it apply RFE immediately?</p>\n<p>Not necessarily.</p>\n<p>A filter method is a more efficient first step.</p>\n<p>For example:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">10,000 terms\n↓\nRemove excessively rare/common terms\n↓\nReduce feature count\n↓\nOptional model-based selection\n</code></pre>\n<p>Why?</p>\n<p>RFE repeatedly trains models, which may be computationally expensive when the number of features is extremely large.</p>\n<p>This scenario appears directly in the lecture's practice section on page 56.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5n-s39\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">31. Scenario 5: A feature has zero variance</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Every observation has a feature Country_Code = 1. Should it be kept?</p>\n<p>Generally, no.</p>\n<p>Because:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$Variance=0$$</div>\n<p>It cannot distinguish between samples in the training dataset.</p>\n<p>The appropriate technique is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{VarianceThreshold}$$</div>\n<p>with an appropriate threshold such as zero.</p>\n<p>However, the feature's inability to vary within the dataset should be distinguished from whether the dataset itself represents the population to which the model will be applied.</p></div></details><h2 class=\"notes-divider\" id=\"l5n-d40\">Part I - Preparing Your Understanding for the Exam</h2><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5n-s41\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">32. High-value distinctions</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>You should be able to explain each of these without referring to notes.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Comparison</th>\n<th>Key reasoning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Scaling vs Selection</td>\n<td>Change numerical values vs remove features</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Standard vs Min-Max</td>\n<td>Mean/SD vs Min/Max</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Standard vs Robust</td>\n<td>Mean/SD vs Median/IQR</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Filter vs Wrapper</td>\n<td>Statistical scoring vs repeated model fitting</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wrapper vs Embedded</td>\n<td>Repeated subset evaluation vs importance during model fitting</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>VarianceThreshold vs Chi-Square</td>\n<td>Variation in $X$ vs relationship between $X$ and $y$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Chi-Square vs RFE</td>\n<td>Individual statistical ranking vs model-dependent recursive elimination</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Feature importance vs causality</td>\n<td>Predictive contribution does not prove causal effect</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Train/test preprocessing</td>\n<td>Learn only from training, reuse on test</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5n-s42\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">33. Check whether you really understand</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p><strong>1. A model uses KNN on Age and Salary. Why might scaling improve it?</strong></p>\n<p>Because distance calculations can be dominated by Salary's larger numerical scale, regardless of whether Salary is more predictive.</p>\n<p><strong>2. A feature contains 99% zeros and 1% ones. Is VarianceThreshold always safe?</strong></p>\n<p>No. It has low variance, but the rare ones may strongly predict an important outcome such as fraud.</p>\n<p><strong>3. Why can Chi-Square and RFE select different feature subsets?</strong></p>\n<p>Chi-Square scores features individually against the target, whereas RFE repeatedly uses a predictive estimator to judge and eliminate features in the context of the current subset.</p>\n<p><strong>4. A Random Forest ranks Age as its most important feature. Does that prove Age causes the outcome?</strong></p>\n<p>No. Feature importance reflects the fitted model's predictive use of that variable, not a causal relationship.</p>\n<p><strong>5. Why should feature selection happen inside cross-validation folds?</strong></p>\n<p>Otherwise feature selection may use information from validation observations, causing an optimistic estimate of generalization performance.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5n-s43\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">34. The entire chapter in one mental model</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Think of the complete ML workflow as:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Raw Data</strong> - Income, age, labels, measurements, text</li>\n<li><strong>Cleaning and Feature Engineering</strong> - Fix data and create useful representations</li>\n<li><strong>Feature Scaling</strong> - Make numerical magnitudes comparable</li>\n<li><strong>Feature Selection</strong> - Keep informative features; reduce unnecessary dimensions</li>\n<li><strong>Model Training and Evaluation</strong> - Measure performance on genuinely unseen data</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Conceptual pipeline. For a real implementation, divide the data into training and test sets before learning scaling parameters or selection rules.</p>\n<p>The deepest lesson in Lecture 5 is not that StandardScaler uses the mean or that RFE repeatedly eliminates features.</p>\n<p>It's that feature representation and feature relevance are two different problems.</p>\n<p>A model can fail because its useful features have incompatible numerical scales.</p>\n<p>It can also fail because it is overwhelmed by irrelevant or redundant variables.</p>\n<p>Scaling addresses the first problem. Selection addresses the second.</p>\n<p>And the only reliable way to know whether either transformation helped is to evaluate the resulting pipeline correctly on data that did not influence its fitting.</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\nBetter\\ Features\n\\neq\nMore\\ Features\n}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\nBetter\\ Features\n=\nAppropriate\\ Scale\n+\nUseful\\ Information\n+\nProper\\ Validation\n}$$</div>\n<p>That is the central logic connecting this chapter to Lectures 1-4.</p></div></details></div></details>");
